OSS Security teams,

We have identified an issue affecting all compilers and interpreters=20
that support Unicode. We believe that the techniques described hereafter=20
can be used to generate adversarial encodings of source code files that=20
can be used to craft targeted attacks against source code that cannot be=20
seen by human reviewers in rendered text. This is of concern to the open=20
source community because, absent defenses, supply chain attacks can be=20
imperceptibly mounted against the ecosystem.

This vulnerability has undergone a coordinated disclosure process that=20
has concluded today. The security advisory can be found at=20
https://trojansource.codes.

Multiple organizations will be releasing parallel security advisories,=20
such as Rust's advisory at=20
https://blog.rust-lang.org/2021/11/01/cve-2021-42574.html, Red Hat's=20
advisory at=20
https://access.redhat.com/security/vulnerabilities/RHSB-2021-007=20
<https://access.redhat.com/security/vulnerabilities/RHSB-2021-007>, and=20
GitHub's advisory at=20
https://github.blog/changelog/2021-10-31-warning-about-bidirectional-unicod=
e-text/=20
<https://github.blog/changelog/2021-10-31-warning-about-bidirectional-unico=
de-text/>.

The attached paper describes an attack paradigm -- which we believe to=20
be novel -- discovered by security researchers at the University of=20
Cambridge. There are two techniques for attack, both of which exploit=20
Unicode's high expressiveness to craft source code files for which=20
rendered text displays divergent logic from the underlying encoded bytes=20
seen by compilers.

The first and primary technique, which we dub the Trojan Source attack,=20
uses Unicode Bidirectional (Bidi) control characters embedded in=20
comments and string literals to produce visually deceptive source code=20
files. This technique enables an adversary to encode constructs that=20
visually appear to be comments or string literals but execute as code,=20
or vice versa. Complete details, as well as recommended mitigations, can=20
be found in the attachment 001 Trojan Source.pdf. This vulnerability is=20
tracked under CVE-2021-42574.

The second technique, to which we refer as the homoglyph variant, uses=20
homoglyphs (characters that render to the same glyph but are represented=20
by different Unicode values) to define adversarial identifiers. In this=20
technique, an adversary defines an identifier such as a function name=20
that appears visually identical to a target function, but is defined=20
using Unicode homoglyphs. This adversarial function then performs some=20
malicious action, then optionally calls the original function it is=20
impersonating. When defined in upstream dependencies such as open source=20
software, these adversarial functions can be imported into downstream=20
software and invoked without visual indication of malicious code.=20
Complete details, as well as recommended mitigations, can also be found=20
in the attachment 001 Trojan Source.pdf. This vulnerability is tracked=20
under CVE-2021-42694.

Proofs-of-concept can be found at=20
https://github.com/nickboucher/trojan-source.

We hope that this information proves useful in building and applying=20
defenses where applicable.

Best,
Nicholas Boucher
University of Cambridge

